WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.744
Chapter 16. Security, privacy, licensing, and minimal exposure. Security boundaries include loopback binding, fixed resource allowlists, input redaction, body caps, strict JSON, CSP, frame denial, same-origin assets, authentication, Origin allowlists, and rate limiting. Public exposure requires additional review.

2
00:00:28.064 --> 00:00:53.994
Local Web binds to 127.0.0.1 and validates Host/Origin; static assets use a fixed allowlist, with no arbitrary file read, path parameter, shell, or command API. Input and context redact common secrets, errors are sanitized, analyses are not stored, and access logs are disabled.

3
00:00:54.314 --> 00:01:17.804
A public MCP token lives only in deployment secrets and must never enter frontend code, logs, documents, manifests, test snapshots, or commits. Origins use an exact allowlist, not a wildcard. Proxy deployments also need trusted client identification and rate limiting.

4
00:01:18.124 --> 00:01:43.164
Code uses the MIT license and documentation uses the project's LICENSE-DOCS. Confirm publication rights for audio, scripts, figures, decks, and downloads before release. Pages should identify themselves as audio interpretations, not full book text, and state copyright ownership.

5
00:01:43.484 --> 00:01:52.301
Loopback, read-only, and no storage by default.

6
00:01:52.621 --> 00:02:01.759
Credentials belong only in environment secrets, never content.

7
00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:11.197
Limit bodies, fields, paths, origins, and request rates.

8
00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:22.699
Before public exposure: TLS, authentication, rate limits, human approval.

9
00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:39.385
Try one step: Use a fictitious token for rejection tests and confirm errors never echo it. Inspect frontend code, logs, manifests, and downloads for real credentials.
